{
  "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
  "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:19 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Stephen E. Eberhardt",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The judges of the Seventh Circuit are unanimous in their belief that this case raises a good question. And, of course, that good question is: Why would Congress want district courts to continue to impose sentences that were universally viewed as unfair and racially discriminatory?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Michael R. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Fair Sentencing Act manifests the requisite fair and necessary implication that Congress intended that its new mandatory minimum thresholds apply in all sentencings after the date of the Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And how -- how long historically, roughly, if you know, does it take from the time the person's caught till the time he's sentenced, when he pleads guilty?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, roughly speaking, there has historically been about 5,000 crack offenders a year."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dreeben",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think that there -- there will probably be thousands of crack defendants who will be sentenced under the old mandatory minimums that Congress repealed because they were perceived as being racially disparate and unfair and -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "New guidelines to what people? That's the issue."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Could I ask you this about your argument? Because I do think the one you're stressing now is a -- is a good argument and your best one. But what troubles me is that an earlier bill, H.R. 265, which contained the provision that says \"there shall be no retroactive application of any portion of this Act\" contains the very language that you're stressing now."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0001-0007",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Government is not asking for reopening of final sentences."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Miguel Estrada",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0001-0006",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think this is a difficult case for public policy but is not a difficult case for legal doctrine. Fairness is on both sides -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Suppose you're wrong about that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Estrada (Amicus)",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0002-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "U.S. v. Smith from the Second Circuit, which -- which was authored by then-Judge Sotomayor. And it was a comparable case in which Congress had dropped the severity of a penalty."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2011-11-5683",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2af923395ca57622ccd6e10694d0287872dfa053087090ce45a9ac5251141d78",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2af923395ca5-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
